\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Profit at expiry per barrel from a call and a put on oil, each with a strike of \$80 and a price of \$4. The call gains when the oil price rises above \$84, the strike plus the price paid, and the put gains when the oil price falls below \$76. If the oil price ends near \$80, either option expires and its holder loses only the \$4. Because the losses are capped and the gains are not, an option is worth more when large price moves in either direction are more likely.}}{107}{figure.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:10-option-payoff}{{7.4}{107}{Profit at expiry per barrel from a call and a put on oil, each with a strike of \$80 and a price of \$4. The call gains when the oil price rises above \$84, the strike plus the price paid, and the put gains when the oil price falls below \$76. If the oil price ends near \$80, either option expires and its holder loses only the \$4. Because the losses are capped and the gains are not, an option is worth more when large price moves in either direction are more likely}{figure.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces Approximate path of the VIX, 1990--2025, drawn through its major peaks and troughs rather than daily data. The VIX measures the volatility of the S\&P~500 over the next 30 days implied by index option prices. It spends long periods near or below its average of about 20 and then spikes sharply during crises, the same volatility clustering seen in past returns, now visible in forward-looking prices. Exact daily values are available from FRED (series VIXCLS).}}{107}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:10-vix}{{7.5}{107}{Approximate path of the VIX, 1990--2025, drawn through its major peaks and troughs rather than daily data. The VIX measures the volatility of the S\&P~500 over the next 30 days implied by index option prices. It spends long periods near or below its average of about 20 and then spikes sharply during crises, the same volatility clustering seen in past returns, now visible in forward-looking prices. Exact daily values are available from FRED (series VIXCLS)}{figure.caption.30}{}}
